    {
      "item_id": "a6ee3143-428c-5d8e-8d8e-d131dc92ac54",
      "session_id": "QA9-who-killed-harriet",
      "trace_id": "d9e9d0cf2fd2a9727346aeef6f8d0d95",
      "output": {
        "question": "Who killed Harriet?",
        "answer": "Harriet Vanger was not killed; she disappeared on September 24, 1966, and was presumed dead until it was revealed years later that she had escaped her uncle Gottfried Vanger's captivity and was alive [E1]. The official account stated that Harriet vanished from Hedeby Island after being last seen by Pastor Otto Falk, with the bridge blocked by an accident preventing anyone from leaving or entering [E2]. It was later discovered that Gottfried Vanger had raped Harriet and held her captive in his cabin on the island before he drowned in 1965, allowing her to escape [E3]. Martin Vanger did not kill Harriet but murdered other women with similar appearances to frame their nephew Henrik for the crimes [E4].",
        "citations": [
          "[E1]",
          "[E2]",
          "[E3]",
          "[E4]"
        ],
        "reranked": [
          {
            "book": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo",
            "book_file": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium book, 1st Vintage Crime_Black Lizard mass-market -- Random House, Incorporated -- isbn13 9780307455352 -- f10e68d0e06407c85b6ba58f6f49d618 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_with_the_Dragon_Tattoo_split_036.html",
            "paragraph": 70,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 26 Tuesday, July 15– Thursday, July 17",
            "page": "35",
            "text": "She was not amused.\n“How many people know the truth?”\n“That you’re alive? Right now, you and me and Anita and my partner. Henrik’s lawyer knows about two-thirds of the story, but he still thinks you died in the sixties.”\nHarriet Vanger seemed to be thinking something over. She stared out at the dark. Mikael once again had an uneasy feeling that he was in a vulnerable situation, and he reminded himself that Harriet Vanger’s own rifle was on a camp bed three paces away. Then he shook himself and stopped imagining things. He changed the subject.",
            "paragraph_end": 73,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "0d4468bc536cd542439231b710c9cadd889ebe48fe63313bed2dd17b003e63a8",
            "id": "0d4468bc-536c-d542-4392-31b710c9cadd",
            "score": 3.8771767616271973,
            "agent_label": "E1"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo",
            "book_file": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium book, 1st Vintage Crime_Black Lizard mass-market -- Random House, Incorporated -- isbn13 9780307455352 -- f10e68d0e06407c85b6ba58f6f49d618 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_with_the_Dragon_Tattoo_split_012.html",
            "paragraph": 19,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Thursday, December 26",
            "page": "11",
            "text": "“At about 2:40 Harriet was in the kitchen here in the house. She drank a glass of milk and talked briefly to Astrid, our cook. They looked out of the window at the commotion down at the bridge.\n“At 2:55 Harriet crossed the courtyard. She was seen by Isabella. About a minute later she ran into Otto Falk, the pastor in Hedeby. At that time the parsonage was where Martin Vanger has his villa today, and the pastor lived on this side of the bridge. He had been in bed, nursing a cold, when the accident took place; he had missed the drama, but someone had telephoned and he was on his way to the bridge. Harriet stopped him on the road and apparently wanted to say something to him, but he waved her off and hurried past. Falk was the last person to see her alive.”",
            "paragraph_end": 20,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "c64047bd60ea6fe74fd6306a792782462c46241fa816079bf010d653aa12da3a",
            "id": "c64047bd-60ea-6fe7-4fd6-306a79278246",
            "score": 3.1024928092956543,
            "agent_label": "E2"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo",
            "book_file": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium book, 1st Vintage Crime_Black Lizard mass-market -- Random House, Incorporated -- isbn13 9780307455352 -- f10e68d0e06407c85b6ba58f6f49d618 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_with_the_Dragon_Tattoo_split_035.html",
            "paragraph": 131,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 25 Saturday, July 12– Monday, July 14",
            "page": "34",
            "text": "“Sometime along the way Harriet discovered that Gottfried had not only committed incest, but he was also a serial sex murderer,” he said.\n“We don’t know when she found out about the murders. It could have been right before Gottfried drowned. It might also have been after he drowned, if he had a diary or had saved press cuttings about them. Something put her on his track.”\n“But that wasn’t what she was threatening to tell Henrik,” Blomkvist said.",
            "paragraph_end": 133,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "b032152944fd3646c497cf0e151836bba1e5dc3ef8943ae9d729eae569110dec",
            "id": "b0321529-44fd-3646-c497-cf0e151836bb",
            "agent_label": "E3"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo",
            "book_file": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium book, 1st Vintage Crime_Black Lizard mass-market -- Random House, Incorporated -- isbn13 9780307455352 -- f10e68d0e06407c85b6ba58f6f49d618 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_with_the_Dragon_Tattoo_split_011.html",
            "paragraph": 184,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 4 Monday, December 23– Thursday, December 26",
            "page": "10",
            "text": "“So he’s the father of Harriet and Martin?” Blomkvist said, pointing at the portrait on the coffee table. Reluctantly he had to admit that the old man’s story was intriguing.\n“Correct. In the late forties Gottfried met a German woman by the name of Isabella Koenig, who had come to Sweden after the war. She was quite a beauty—I mean that she had a lovely radiance like Garbo or Ingrid Bergman. Harriet probably got more of her genes from her mother rather than from Gottfried. As you can see from the photograph, she was pretty even at fourteen.”",
            "paragraph_end": 185,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
